ses of dementia** if diagnosed early. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 92-year-old woman is evaluated for progressive gait instability, cognitive decline, and urinary urgency/incontinence over the past 2 months. Physical exam reveals a **magnetic** gait pattern. Brain MRI shows **ventriculomegaly** out of proportion to cortical sulcal atrophy. Which of the following is the most appropriate next diagnostic step?', '["Large-volume lumbar puncture (removing 30-50 mL CSF) with pre- and post-procedure gait assessment and cognitive testing \u2014 improvement predicts successful treatment with ventriculoperitoneal (VP) shunt placement", "Initiation of high-dose levodopa/carbidopa for 6 months before considering any surgical intervention", "Trial of high-dose furosemide to reduce CSF production and relieve ventricular pressure", "Emergent external ventricular drain placement due to acute obstructive hydrocephalus", "Brain biopsy of the periventricular region to rule out lymphoma or gliomatosis cerebri"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This patient presents with the classic triad of **Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus (NPH)** — ''''wet, wobbly, and wacky'''': (1) **Gait disturbance** (typically the earliest and most prominent symptom) — characteristically a **magnetic** gait (feet seem glued to the floor), broad-based, with short shuffling steps; (2) **Cognitive decline** — subcortical pattern with executive dysfunction, psychomotor slowing, and impaired attention; (3) **Urinary incontinence** — typically urgency-type initially, progressing to frank incontinence. MRI shows **ventriculomegaly** (Evans'''' index >0.3) **disproportionate to sulcal atrophy** (distinguishing it from ex-vacuo hydrocephalus of neurodegeneration). The diagnostic and prognostic test is a **large-volume LP** (''''tap test''''), removing 30-50 mL of CSF and measuring gait speed and cognition before and after. Improvement in gait (the most responsive symptom) predicts a favorable response to definitive treatment: **VP shunt placement**. NPH is one of the few **reversible causes of dementia** if diagnosed early. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old male presents with a 5-month history of memory difficulty, poor concentration, and low energy. During cognitive testing, the patient frequently responds **''''I don''''t know''''** and makes minimal effort, but can complete tasks when encouraged. The patient appears tearful and expresses significant distress about his memory. His spouse reports anhedonia, 10-pound weight loss, and early morning awakenings. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Malingering \u2014 the patient is intentionally faking cognitive impairment for secondary gain", "Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease \u2014 the subacute onset over months suggests prion disease as the primary etiology", "Frontotemporal dementia \u2014 weight loss and apathy in the elderly always indicate frontotemporal degeneration rather than depression", "Pseudodementia (depression-related cognitive impairment) \u2014 cognitive deficits secondary to major depressive disorder; characterized by poor effort on testing, ''''I don''''t know'''' responses, acute/subacute onset, and marked distress about cognitive symptoms; fully reversible with antidepressant treatment", "Alzheimer''''s disease \u2014 patients with AD typically express great distress and awareness of their memory impairment and give ''''I don''''t know'''' responses on testing"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Pseudodementia** (depressive cognitive impairment) refers to cognitive deficits occurring secondary to **major depressive disorder** in older adults. Key features distinguishing pseudodementia from true neurodegenerative dementia: PSEUDODEMENTIA: (1) **Acute or subacute onset** (weeks to months), often with identifiable precipitant; (2) **''''I don''''t know'''' responses** — poor effort, gives up easily, but CAN perform when encouraged; (3) **Marked distress** about cognitive symptoms (patients with true dementia often minimize or are unaware); (4) **Vegetative symptoms** — anhedonia, weight change, sleep disturbance, fatigue; (5) **History of depression**. TRUE DEMENTIA: (1) Insidious onset over years; (2) Patients try to answer but make errors (''''near-miss'''' confabulations); (3) Often minimize or deny deficits; (4) Intact affect early on. Treatment: **SSRI** (sertraline or escitalopram) or **mirtazapine** (if appetite stimulation and sleep aid needed — avoid TCAs per Beers Criteria due to anticholinergic burden). Cognitive deficits are **fully reversible** with depression treatment. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
