    (v_topic_id, 'A 86-year-old woman presents with a 8-month history of memory difficulty, poor concentration, and low energy. During cognitive testing, the patient frequently responds **''''I don''''t know''''** and makes minimal effort, but can complete tasks when encouraged. The patient appears tearful and expresses significant distress about her memory. Her spouse reports anhedonia, 10-pound weight loss, and early morning awakenings. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease \u2014 the subacute onset over months suggests prion disease as the primary etiology", "Pseudodementia (depression-related cognitive impairment) \u2014 cognitive deficits secondary to major depressive disorder; characterized by poor effort on testing, ''''I don''''t know'''' responses, acute/subacute onset, and marked distress about cognitive symptoms; fully reversible with antidepressant treatment", "Malingering \u2014 the patient is intentionally faking cognitive impairment for secondary gain", "Frontotemporal dementia \u2014 weight loss and apathy in the elderly always indicate frontotemporal degeneration rather than depression", "Alzheimer''''s disease \u2014 patients with AD typically express great distress and awareness of their memory impairment and give ''''I don''''t know'''' responses on testing"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Pseudodementia** (depressive cognitive impairment) refers to cognitive deficits occurring secondary to **major depressive disorder** in older adults. Key features distinguishing pseudodementia from true neurodegenerative dementia: PSEUDODEMENTIA: (1) **Acute or subacute onset** (weeks to months), often with identifiable precipitant; (2) **''''I don''''t know'''' responses** — poor effort, gives up easily, but CAN perform when encouraged; (3) **Marked distress** about cognitive symptoms (patients with true dementia often minimize or are unaware); (4) **Vegetative symptoms** — anhedonia, weight change, sleep disturbance, fatigue; (5) **History of depression**. TRUE DEMENTIA: (1) Insidious onset over years; (2) Patients try to answer but make errors (''''near-miss'''' confabulations); (3) Often minimize or deny deficits; (4) Intact affect early on. Treatment: **SSRI** (sertraline or escitalopram) or **mirtazapine** (if appetite stimulation and sleep aid needed — avoid TCAs per Beers Criteria due to anticholinergic burden). Cognitive deficits are **fully reversible** with depression treatment. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
