    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-year-old woman presents with a 7-month history of memory difficulty, poor concentration, and low energy. During cognitive testing, the patient frequently responds **''''I don''''t know''''** and makes minimal effort, but can complete tasks when encouraged. The patient appears tearful and expresses significant distress about her memory. Her spouse reports anhedonia, 10-pound weight loss, and early morning awakenings. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease \u2014 the subacute onset over months suggests prion disease as the primary etiology", "Pseudodementia (depression-related cognitive impairment) \u2014 cognitive deficits secondary to major depressive disorder; characterized by poor effort on testing, ''''I don''''t know'''' responses, acute/subacute onset, and marked distress about cognitive symptoms; fully reversible with antidepressant treatment", "Malingering \u2014 the patient is intentionally faking cognitive impairment for secondary gain", "Frontotemporal dementia \u2014 weight loss and apathy in the elderly always indicate frontotemporal degeneration rather than depression", "Alzheimer''''s disease \u2014 patients with AD typically express great distress and awareness of their memory impairment and give ''''I don''''t know'''' responses on testing"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Pseudodementia** (depressive cognitive impairment) refers to cognitive deficits occurring secondary to **major depressive disorder** in older adults. Key features distinguishing pseudodementia from true neurodegenerative dementia: PSEUDODEMENTIA: (1) **Acute or subacute onset** (weeks to months), often with identifiable precipitant; (2) **''''I don''''t know'''' responses** — poor effort, gives up easily, but CAN perform when encouraged; (3) **Marked distress** about cognitive symptoms (patients with true dementia often minimize or are unaware); (4) **Vegetative symptoms** — anhedonia, weight change, sleep disturbance, fatigue; (5) **History of depression**. TRUE DEMENTIA: (1) Insidious onset over years; (2) Patients try to answer but make errors (''''near-miss'''' confabulations); (3) Often minimize or deny deficits; (4) Intact affect early on. Treatment: **SSRI** (sertraline or escitalopram) or **mirtazapine** (if appetite stimulation and sleep aid needed — avoid TCAs per Beers Criteria due to anticholinergic burden). Cognitive deficits are **fully reversible** with depression treatment. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 86-year-old man is admitted due to an alarmingly **rapid cognitive decline** over the past 4 weeks, accompanied by cerebellar ataxia, cortical visual disturbances, and frequent involuntary muscle jerks (**startle myoclonus**). EEG shows **generalized periodic discharges**. Brain MRI with DWI demonstrates **restricted diffusion in the cortical ribbon and basal ganglia**. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis and confirmatory test?', '["Rapidly progressive Alzheimer''''s disease \u2014 confirmed by low CSF A\u03b242 and treated with high-dose memantine", "Autoimmune encephalitis \u2014 confirmed by serum VDRL and treated with IV penicillin", "Status epilepticus \u2014 the EEG findings and myoclonus indicate seizure activity requiring emergent benzodiazepines and phenytoin", "Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease (CJD) \u2014 a rapidly fatal prion disease; confirmed by CSF RT-QuIC assay (real-time quaking-induced conversion) or 14-3-3 protein, with characteristic MRI (cortical ribboning on DWI) and EEG (periodic sharp wave complexes)", "Viral encephalitis (HSV) \u2014 confirmed by CSF HSV PCR and treated with IV acyclovir; would show temporal lobe enhancement, not cortical ribboning"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease (CJD)** is the most common human prion disease, caused by the conformational conversion of normal cellular prion protein (PrPc) into a pathogenic, protease-resistant form (PrPsc), leading to **spongiform neurodegeneration**. Key diagnostic features: (1) **Rapidly progressive dementia** — decline over **weeks to months** (NOT years); (2) **Startle myoclonus** — sudden jerks triggered by noise or touch; (3) **Cerebellar ataxia** — gait unsteadiness, dysmetria; (4) **Cortical visual disturbance** — Heidenhain variant; (5) **EEG: periodic sharp wave complexes** (or triphasic waves); (6) **MRI: cortical ribboning** (restricted diffusion along the cortical ribbon on DWI) and **basal ganglia hyperintensity** on DWI/FLAIR — the most sensitive MRI finding; (7) **CSF: RT-QuIC** (real-time quaking-induced conversion) — highest sensitivity and specificity (~92% and ~100%); **14-3-3 protein** — elevated but less specific. CJD is **uniformly fatal** (median survival 5-12 months). There is no treatment; care is **supportive/palliative**. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 67-year-old female is admitted due to an alarmingly **rapid cognitive decline** over the past 4 weeks, accompanied by cerebellar ataxia, cortical visual disturbances, and frequent involuntary muscle jerks (**startle myoclonus**). EEG shows **generalized periodic discharges**. Brain MRI with DWI demonstrates **restricted diffusion in the cortical ribbon and basal ganglia**. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis and confirmatory test?', '["Autoimmune encephalitis \u2014 confirmed by serum VDRL and treated with IV penicillin", "Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease (CJD) \u2014 a rapidly fatal prion disease; confirmed by CSF RT-QuIC assay (real-time quaking-induced conversion) or 14-3-3 protein, with characteristic MRI (cortical ribboning on DWI) and EEG (periodic sharp wave complexes)", "Rapidly progressive Alzheimer''''s disease \u2014 confirmed by low CSF A\u03b242 and treated with high-dose memantine", "Status epilepticus \u2014 the EEG findings and myoclonus indicate seizure activity requiring emergent benzodiazepines and phenytoin", "Viral encephalitis (HSV) \u2014 confirmed by CSF HSV PCR and treated with IV acyclovir; would show temporal lobe enhancement, not cortical ribboning"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease (CJD)** is the most common human prion disease, caused by the conformational conversion of normal cellular prion protein (PrPc) into a pathogenic, protease-resistant form (PrPsc), leading to **spongiform neurodegeneration**. Key diagnostic features: (1) **Rapidly progressive dementia** — decline over **weeks to months** (NOT years); (2) **Startle myoclonus** — sudden jerks triggered by noise or touch; (3) **Cerebellar ataxia** — gait unsteadiness, dysmetria; (4) **Cortical visual disturbance** — Heidenhain variant; (5) **EEG: periodic sharp wave complexes** (or triphasic waves); (6) **MRI: cortical ribboning** (restricted diffusion along the cortical ribbon on DWI) and **basal ganglia hyperintensity** on DWI/FLAIR — the most sensitive MRI finding; (7) **CSF: RT-QuIC** (real-time quaking-induced conversion) — highest sensitivity and specificity (~92% and ~100%); **14-3-3 protein** — elevated but less specific. CJD is **uniformly fatal** (median survival 5-12 months). There is no treatment; care is **supportive/palliative**. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 76-year-old woman is admitted due to an alarmingly **rapid cognitive decline** over the past 2 weeks, accompanied by cerebellar ataxia, cortical visual disturbances, and frequent involuntary muscle jerks (**startle myoclonus**). EEG shows **generalized periodic discharges**. Brain MRI with DWI demonstrates **restricted diffusion in the cortical ribbon and basal ganglia**. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis and confirmatory test?', '["Viral encephalitis (HSV) \u2014 confirmed by CSF HSV PCR and treated with IV acyclovir; would show temporal lobe enhancement, not cortical ribboning", "Autoimmune encephalitis \u2014 confirmed by serum VDRL and treated with IV penicillin", "Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease (CJD) \u2014 a rapidly fatal prion disease; confirmed by CSF RT-QuIC assay (real-time quaking-induced conversion) or 14-3-3 protein, with characteristic MRI (cortical ribboning on DWI) and EEG (periodic sharp wave complexes)", "Status epilepticus \u2014 the EEG findings and myoclonus indicate seizure activity requiring emergent benzodiazepines and phenytoin", "Rapidly progressive Alzheimer''''s disease \u2014 confirmed by low CSF A\u03b242 and treated with high-dose memantine"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease (CJD)** is the most common human prion disease, caused by the conformational conversion of normal cellular prion protein (PrPc) into a pathogenic, protease-resistant form (PrPsc), leading to **spongiform neurodegeneration**. Key diagnostic features: (1) **Rapidly progressive dementia** — decline over **weeks to months** (NOT years); (2) **Startle myoclonus** — sudden jerks triggered by noise or touch; (3) **Cerebellar ataxia** — gait unsteadiness, dysmetria; (4) **Cortical visual disturbance** — Heidenhain variant; (5) **EEG: periodic sharp wave complexes** (or triphasic waves); (6) **MRI: cortical ribboning** (restricted diffusion along the cortical ribbon on DWI) and **basal ganglia hyperintensity** on DWI/FLAIR — the most sensitive MRI finding; (7) **CSF: RT-QuIC** (real-time quaking-induced conversion) — highest sensitivity and specificity (~92% and ~100%); **14-3-3 protein** — elevated but less specific. CJD is **uniformly fatal** (median survival 5-12 months). There is no treatment; care is **supportive/palliative**. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 70-year-old woman is admitted due to an alarmingly **rapid cognitive decline** over the past 6 weeks, accompanied by cerebellar ataxia, cortical visual disturbances, and frequent involuntary muscle jerks (**startle myoclonus**). EEG shows **periodic sharp wave complexes**. Brain MRI with DWI demonstrates **restricted diffusion in the cortical ribbon and basal ganglia**. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis and confirmatory test?', '["Viral encephalitis (HSV) \u2014 confirmed by CSF HSV PCR and treated with IV acyclovir; would show temporal lobe enhancement, not cortical ribboning", "Autoimmune encephalitis \u2014 confirmed by serum VDRL and treated with IV penicillin", "Rapidly progressive Alzheimer''''s disease \u2014 confirmed by low CSF A\u03b242 and treated with high-dose memantine", "Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease (CJD) \u2014 a rapidly fatal prion disease; confirmed by CSF RT-QuIC assay (real-time quaking-induced conversion) or 14-3-3 protein, with characteristic MRI (cortical ribboning on DWI) and EEG (periodic sharp wave complexes)", "Status epilepticus \u2014 the EEG findings and myoclonus indicate seizure activity requiring emergent benzodiazepines and phenytoin"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease (CJD)** is the most common human prion disease, caused by the conformational conversion of normal cellular prion protein (PrPc) into a pathogenic, protease-resistant form (PrPsc), leading to **spongiform neurodegeneration**. Key diagnostic features: (1) **Rapidly progressive dementia** — decline over **weeks to months** (NOT years); (2) **Startle myoclonus** — sudden jerks triggered by noise or touch; (3) **Cerebellar ataxia** — gait unsteadiness, dysmetria; (4) **Cortical visual disturbance** — Heidenhain variant; (5) **EEG: periodic sharp wave complexes** (or triphasic waves); (6) **MRI: cortical ribboning** (restricted diffusion along the cortical ribbon on DWI) and **basal ganglia hyperintensity** on DWI/FLAIR — the most s