    (v_topic_id, 'A 71-year-old male is admitted due to an alarmingly **rapid cognitive decline** over the past 2 weeks, accompanied by cerebellar ataxia, cortical visual disturbances, and frequent involuntary muscle jerks (**startle myoclonus**). EEG shows **periodic sharp wave complexes**. Brain MRI with DWI demonstrates **restricted diffusion in the cortical ribbon and basal ganglia**. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis and confirmatory test?', '["Status epilepticus \u2014 the EEG findings and myoclonus indicate seizure activity requiring emergent benzodiazepines and phenytoin", "Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease (CJD) \u2014 a rapidly fatal prion disease; confirmed by CSF RT-QuIC assay (real-time quaking-induced conversion) or 14-3-3 protein, with characteristic MRI (cortical ribboning on DWI) and EEG (periodic sharp wave complexes)", "Autoimmune encephalitis \u2014 confirmed by serum VDRL and treated with IV penicillin", "Viral encephalitis (HSV) \u2014 confirmed by CSF HSV PCR and treated with IV acyclovir; would show temporal lobe enhancement, not cortical ribboning", "Rapidly progressive Alzheimer''''s disease \u2014 confirmed by low CSF A\u03b242 and treated with high-dose memantine"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease (CJD)** is the most common human prion disease, caused by the conformational conversion of normal cellular prion protein (PrPc) into a pathogenic, protease-resistant form (PrPsc), leading to **spongiform neurodegeneration**. Key diagnostic features: (1) **Rapidly progressive dementia** — decline over **weeks to months** (NOT years); (2) **Startle myoclonus** — sudden jerks triggered by noise or touch; (3) **Cerebellar ataxia** — gait unsteadiness, dysmetria; (4) **Cortical visual disturbance** — Heidenhain variant; (5) **EEG: periodic sharp wave complexes** (or triphasic waves); (6) **MRI: cortical ribboning** (restricted diffusion along the cortical ribbon on DWI) and **basal ganglia hyperintensity** on DWI/FLAIR — the most sensitive MRI finding; (7) **CSF: RT-QuIC** (real-time quaking-induced conversion) — highest sensitivity and specificity (~92% and ~100%); **14-3-3 protein** — elevated but less specific. CJD is **uniformly fatal** (median survival 5-12 months). There is no treatment; care is **supportive/palliative**. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 69-year-old man is admitted due to an alarmingly **rapid cognitive decline** over the past 6 weeks, accompanied by cerebellar ataxia, cortical visual disturbances, and frequent involuntary muscle jerks (**startle myoclonus**). EEG shows **generalized periodic discharges**. Brain MRI with DWI demonstrates **restricted diffusion in the cortical ribbon and basal ganglia**. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis and confirmatory test?', '["Rapidly progressive Alzheimer''''s disease \u2014 confirmed by low CSF A\u03b242 and treated with high-dose memantine", "Status epilepticus \u2014 the EEG findings and myoclonus indicate seizure activity requiring emergent benzodiazepines and phenytoin", "Autoimmune encephalitis \u2014 confirmed by serum VDRL and treated with IV penicillin", "Viral encephalitis (HSV) \u2014 confirmed by CSF HSV PCR and treated with IV acyclovir; would show temporal lobe enhancement, not cortical ribboning", "Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease (CJD) \u2014 a rapidly fatal prion disease; confirmed by CSF RT-QuIC assay (real-time quaking-induced conversion) or 14-3-3 protein, with characteristic MRI (cortical ribboning on DWI) and EEG (periodic sharp wave complexes)"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease (CJD)** is the most common human prion disease, caused by the conformational conversion of normal cellular prion protein (PrPc) into a pathogenic, protease-resistant form (PrPsc), leading to **spongiform neurodegeneration**. Key diagnostic features: (1) **Rapidly progressive dementia** — decline over **weeks to months** (NOT years); (2) **Startle myoclonus** — sudden jerks triggered by noise or touch; (3) **Cerebellar ataxia** — gait unsteadiness, dysmetria; (4) **Cortical visual disturbance** — Heidenhain variant; (5) **EEG: periodic sharp wave complexes** (or triphasic waves); (6) **MRI: cortical ribboning** (restricted diffusion along the cortical ribbon on DWI) and **basal ganglia hyperintensity** on DWI/FLAIR — the most sensitive MRI finding; (7) **CSF: RT-QuIC** (real-time quaking-induced conversion) — highest sensitivity and specificity (~92% and ~100%); **14-3-3 protein** — elevated but less specific. CJD is **uniformly fatal** (median survival 5-12 months). There is no treatment; care is **supportive/palliative**. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 71-year-old man is admitted due to an alarmingly **rapid cognitive decline** over the past 4 weeks, accompanied by cerebellar ataxia, cortical visual disturbances, and frequent involuntary muscle jerks (**startle myoclonus**). EEG shows **periodic sharp wave complexes**. Brain MRI with DWI demonstrates **restricted diffusion in the cortical ribbon and basal ganglia**. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis and confirmatory test?', '["Autoimmune encephalitis \u2014 confirmed by serum VDRL and treated with IV penicillin", "Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease (CJD) \u2014 a rapidly fatal prion disease; confirmed by CSF RT-QuIC assay (real-time quaking-induced conversion) or 14-3-3 protein, with characteristic MRI (cortical ribboning on DWI) and EEG (periodic sharp wave complexes)", "Status epilepticus \u2014 the EEG findings and myoclonus indicate seizure activity requiring emergent benzodiazepines and phenytoin", "Rapidly progressive Alzheimer''''s disease \u2014 confirmed by low CSF A\u03b242 and treated with high-dose memantine", "Viral encephalitis (HSV) \u2014 confirmed by CSF HSV PCR and treated with IV acyclovir; would show temporal lobe enhancement, not cortical ribboning"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease (CJD)** is the most common human prion disease, caused by the conformational conversion of normal cellular prion protein (PrPc) into a pathogenic, protease-resistant form (PrPsc), leading to **spongiform neurodegeneration**. Key diagnostic features: (1) **Rapidly progressive dementia** — decline over **weeks to months** (NOT years); (2) **Startle myoclonus** — sudden jerks triggered by noise or touch; (3) **Cerebellar ataxia** — gait unsteadiness, dysmetria; (4) **Cortical visual disturbance** — Heidenhain variant; (5) **EEG: periodic sharp wave complexes** (or triphasic waves); (6) **MRI: cortical ribboning** (restricted diffusion along the cortical ribbon on DWI) and **basal ganglia hyperintensity** on DWI/FLAIR — the most sensitive MRI finding; (7) **CSF: RT-QuIC** (real-time quaking-induced conversion) — highest sensitivity and specificity (~92% and ~100%); **14-3-3 protein** — elevated but less specific. CJD is **uniformly fatal** (median survival 5-12 months). There is no treatment; care is **supportive/palliative**. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 75-year-old woman is admitted due to an alarmingly **rapid cognitive decline** over the past 2 weeks, accompanied by cerebellar ataxia, cortical visual disturbances, and frequent involuntary muscle jerks (**startle myoclonus**). EEG shows **triphasic periodic complexes**. Brain MRI with DWI demonstrates **restricted diffusion in the cortical ribbon and basal ganglia**. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis and confirmatory test?', '["Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease (CJD) \u2014 a rapidly fatal prion disease; confirmed by CSF RT-QuIC assay (real-time quaking-induced conversion) or 14-3-3 protein, with characteristic MRI (cortical ribboning on DWI) and EEG (periodic sharp wave complexes)", "Status epilepticus \u2014 the EEG findings and myoclonus indicate seizure activity requiring emergent benzodiazepines and phenytoin", "Rapidly progressive Alzheimer''''s disease \u2014 confirmed by low CSF A\u03b242 and treated with high-dose memantine", "Autoimmune encephalitis \u2014 confirmed by serum VDRL and treated with IV penicillin", "Viral encephalitis (HSV) \u2014 confirmed by CSF HSV PCR and treated with IV acyclovir; would show temporal lobe enhancement, not cortical ribboning"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease (CJD)** is the most common human prion disease, caused by the conformational conversion of normal cellular prion protein (PrPc) into a pathogenic, protease-resistant form (PrPsc), leading to **spongiform neurodegeneration**. Key diagnostic features: (1) **Rapidly progressive dementia** — decline over **weeks to months** (NOT years); (2) **Startle myoclonus** — sudden jerks triggered by noise or touch; (3) **Cerebellar ataxia** — gait unsteadiness, dysmetria; (4) **Cortical visual disturbance** — Heidenhain variant; (5) **EEG: periodic sharp wave complexes** (or triphasic waves); (6) **MRI: cortical ribboning** (restricted diffusion along the cortical ribbon on DWI) and **basal ganglia hyperintensity** on DWI/FLAIR — the most sensitive MRI finding; (7) **CSF: RT-QuIC** (real-time quaking-induced conversion) — highest sensitivity and specificity (~92% and ~100%); **14-3-3 protein** — elevated but less specific. CJD is **uniformly fatal** (median survival 5-12 months). There is no treatment; care is **supportive/palliative**. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 74-year-old male with known moderate Alzheimer''''s disease is brought to the emergency department due to an **acute change** in mental status over the past 24 hours. At baseline, the patient was conversational and ambulatory with assistance. Now the patient is inattentive, disoriented, agitated, and having visual hallucinations that wax and wane. Vitals show temperature 38.8°C, HR 97, and oxygen saturation 93%. Which of the following is the MOST important next step?', '["Evaluate for an underlying medical cause of delirium (infection, medications, metabolic derangement) using the Confusion Assessment Method (CAM) to confirm delirium; obtain urinalysis, chest X-ray, CBC, BMP, and review the medication list for deliriogenic drugs", "Obtain an emergent brain MRI to rule out acute stroke, as the acute change in cognition indicates a large vessel occlusion", "Reassure the family that fluctuating confusion is a normal feature of moderate Alzheimer''''s disease and does not require workup", "Administer haloperidol 10 mg IV for acute psychosis, as the visual hallucinations indicate a new psychiatric disorder", "Increase the donepezil dose to treat the acute worsening of Alzheimer''''s disease symptoms"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Delirium superimposed on dementia** is extremely common (affects up to 50% of hospitalized dementia patients) and is a medical emergency. Key distinguishing features: DELIRIUM: **acute** onset (hours to days), **fluctuating** course, **inattention** (cardinal feature), altered level of consciousness, often **reversible** once the underlying cause is treated. DEMENTIA: **insidious** onset (months to years), **progressive** course, **preserved attention** (until late stages), clear consciousness. When a patient with known dementia develops an **acute change** from baseline, always assume **delirium until proven otherwise**. The **Confusion Assessment Method (CAM)** is the standard screening tool (requires acute onset + inattention + EITHER disorganized thinking OR altered consciousness). Workup must identify the underlying cause: (1) **Infection** (UTI, pneumonia) — most common precipitant; (2) **Medications** (anticholinergics, benzodiazepines, opioids); (3) **Metabolic** (electrolyte abnormalities, uremia, hepatic encephalopathy); (4) **Pain, constipation, urinary retention**. Treatment is treating the underlying cause. AVOID antipsychotics unless patient is dangerous. (Ref: AGS Beers Criteria; Hazzard''''s Ch. 71; Inouye Delirium Prevention)'),
