    (v_topic_id, 'A 87-year-old man with known moderate Alzheimer''''s disease is brought to the emergency department due to an **acute change** in mental status over the past 24 hours. At baseline, the patient was conversational and ambulatory with assistance. Now the patient is inattentive, disoriented, agitated, and having visual hallucinations that wax and wane. Vitals show temperature 38.8°C, HR 102, and oxygen saturation 93%. Which of the following is the MOST important next step?', '["Reassure the family that fluctuating confusion is a normal feature of moderate Alzheimer''''s disease and does not require workup", "Obtain an emergent brain MRI to rule out acute stroke, as the acute change in cognition indicates a large vessel occlusion", "Evaluate for an underlying medical cause of delirium (infection, medications, metabolic derangement) using the Confusion Assessment Method (CAM) to confirm delirium; obtain urinalysis, chest X-ray, CBC, BMP, and review the medication list for deliriogenic drugs", "Administer haloperidol 10 mg IV for acute psychosis, as the visual hallucinations indicate a new psychiatric disorder", "Increase the donepezil dose to treat the acute worsening of Alzheimer''''s disease symptoms"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Delirium superimposed on dementia** is extremely common (affects up to 50% of hospitalized dementia patients) and is a medical emergency. Key distinguishing features: DELIRIUM: **acute** onset (hours to days), **fluctuating** course, **inattention** (cardinal feature), altered level of consciousness, often **reversible** once the underlying cause is treated. DEMENTIA: **insidious** onset (months to years), **progressive** course, **preserved attention** (until late stages), clear consciousness. When a patient with known dementia develops an **acute change** from baseline, always assume **delirium until proven otherwise**. The **Confusion Assessment Method (CAM)** is the standard screening tool (requires acute onset + inattention + EITHER disorganized thinking OR altered consciousness). Workup must identify the underlying cause: (1) **Infection** (UTI, pneumonia) — most common precipitant; (2) **Medications** (anticholinergics, benzodiazepines, opioids); (3) **Metabolic** (electrolyte abnormalities, uremia, hepatic encephalopathy); (4) **Pain, constipation, urinary retention**. Treatment is treating the underlying cause. AVOID antipsychotics unless patient is dangerous. (Ref: AGS Beers Criteria; Hazzard''''s Ch. 71; Inouye Delirium Prevention)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'An 68-year-old woman with a history of overactive bladder, insomnia, and chronic back pain is evaluated for a 8-month history of progressive confusion, memory impairment, and visual hallucinations. Current medications include oxybutynin 5 mg TID, diphenhydramine 50 mg nightly, and diazepam 5 mg TID. The patient was cognitively normal prior to starting these medications. Which of the following medications is the MOST important to discontinue first, and why?', '["Discontinue only oxybutynin and replace with a higher dose of diphenhydramine for both bladder symptoms and sleep, as diphenhydramine is the safest medication in elderly patients", "All three medications have high anticholinergic burden or sedative properties and should be deprescribed; oxybutynin and diphenhydramine are strongly anticholinergic (Beers Criteria ''''avoid'''') and can cause cognitive impairment, confusion, and hallucinations; diazepam (long-acting benzodiazepine) increases fall and fracture risk \u2014 all should be tapered and replaced with safer alternatives", "Continue all current medications, as drug-induced cognitive impairment is extremely rare in elderly patients and these symptoms indicate early Alzheimer''''s disease", "Discontinue only diazepam and replace with lorazepam 2 mg TID, as shorter-acting benzodiazepines have no cognitive side effects in the elderly", "Add donepezil to counteract the cognitive side effects of the current medications while continuing all three drugs unchanged"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Medication-induced cognitive impairment** is a common and **reversible** cause of confusion in elderly patients. This patient is on THREE high-risk medications per the **AGS Beers Criteria**: (1) **Oxybutynin** — a first-generation antimuscarinic with HIGH **anticholinergic burden**; crosses the blood-brain barrier and is strongly associated with cognitive impairment, confusion, delirium, and hallucinations. Safer alternatives: mirabegron (β3-agonist, no anticholinergic effects) or behavioral bladder training; (2) **Diphenhydramine** — a first-generation antihistamine with STRONG anticholinergic properties; Beers Criteria: **AVOID** in elderly (causes sedation, cognitive impairment, delirium, falls). Safer sleep alternative: sleep hygiene, melatonin, trazodone; (3) **Diazepam** — a long-acting benzodiazepine (half-life ~100 hours in elderly); Beers Criteria: **AVOID** all benzodiazepines in elderly (increased risk of falls, fractures, cognitive impairment, paradoxical agitation). The **Anticholinergic Cognitive Burden (ACB) Scale** can quantify cumulative anticholinergic load. Adding donepezil to counteract anticholinergics is inappropriate — the correct approach is to **stop the offending agents**. (Ref: AGS Beers Criteria 2023; Hazzard''''s Ch. 73)'),
