ic effects) or behavioral bladder training; (2) **Diphenhydramine** — a first-generation antihistamine with STRONG anticholinergic properties; Beers Criteria: **AVOID** in elderly (causes sedation, cognitive impairment, delirium, falls). Safer sleep alternative: sleep hygiene, melatonin, trazodone; (3) **Diazepam** — a long-acting benzodiazepine (half-life ~100 hours in elderly); Beers Criteria: **AVOID** all benzodiazepines in elderly (increased risk of falls, fractures, cognitive impairment, paradoxical agitation). The **Anticholinergic Cognitive Burden (ACB) Scale** can quantify cumulative anticholinergic load. Adding donepezil to counteract anticholinergics is inappropriate — the correct approach is to **stop the offending agents**. (Ref: AGS Beers Criteria 2023; Hazzard''''s Ch. 73)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 92-year-old woman with a history of chronic alcohol use disorder and poor nutritional status presents with acute confusion, horizontal nystagmus, bilateral lateral rectus palsy (CN VI), and unsteady gait. After treatment of the acute phase, the patient develops severe anterograde amnesia (unable to form new memories) and begins **confabulating** (fabricating elaborate stories to fill memory gaps without awareness of doing so). Which of the following is the most important initial treatment, and what complication must be avoided?', '["Oral folate supplementation alone is sufficient to reverse both the acute encephalopathy and the chronic amnesia", "IV thiamine (vitamin B1) must be administered BEFORE any glucose-containing fluids \u2014 glucose metabolism consumes thiamine, and giving glucose first can precipitate or worsen Wernicke encephalopathy; the acute phase (Wernicke) is reversible but the chronic amnestic phase (Korsakoff syndrome) is often permanent", "High-dose IV corticosteroids to reduce cerebral edema from alcohol-related neuroinflammation", "IV dextrose 50% (D50) should be given first to treat presumed hypoglycemia, followed by oral thiamine supplementation at discharge", "Emergent MRI-guided brain biopsy of the mammillary bodies to confirm the diagnosis before initiating any treatment"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This patient has **Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome**, caused by **thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency** — most commonly due to chronic alcohol use with malnutrition, but also seen in elderly patients with malabsorption, prolonged vomiting, or anorexia. **Wernicke encephalopathy** (acute phase) presents with the classic triad: (1) **Encephalopathy** (confusion, altered mental status); (2) **Oculomotor dysfunction** (nystagmus, lateral rectus palsy/CN VI, conjugate gaze palsy); (3) **Cerebellar ataxia** (wide-based, unsteady gait). Only ~16% present with the complete triad. **Korsakoff syndrome** (chronic phase) develops if Wernicke''''s is untreated or inadequately treated: **anterograde amnesia** (cannot form new memories), **retrograde amnesia**, and **confabulation** (filling memory gaps with fabricated stories without awareness). CRITICAL: **IV thiamine MUST be given BEFORE glucose**. Glucose metabolism requires thiamine as a cofactor; administering glucose to a thiamine-depleted patient diverts the remaining thiamine stores, potentially precipitating or worsening Wernicke encephalopathy. Wernicke''''s is **reversible** with prompt IV thiamine; Korsakoff''''s is often **irreversible** (~20% recovery). (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 80-year-old male with a history of chronic alcohol use disorder and poor nutritional status presents with acute confusion, horizontal nystagmus, bilateral lateral rectus palsy (CN VI), and unsteady gait. After treatment of the acute phase, the patient develops severe anterograde amnesia (unable to form new memories) and begins **confabulating** (fabricating elaborate stories to fill memory gaps without awareness of doing so). Which of the following is the most important initial treatment, and what complication must be avoided?', '["Oral folate supplementation alone is sufficient to reverse both the acute encephalopathy and the chronic amnesia", "High-dose IV corticosteroids to reduce cerebral edema from alcohol-related neuroinflammation", "Emergent MRI-guided brain biopsy of the mammillary bodies to confirm the diagnosis before initiating any treatment", "IV dextrose 50% (D50) should be given first to treat presumed hypoglycemia, followed by oral thiamine supplementation at discharge", "IV thiamine (vitamin B1) must be administered BEFORE any glucose-containing fluids \u2014 glucose metabolism consumes thiamine, and giving glucose first can precipitate or worsen Wernicke encephalopathy; the acute phase (Wernicke) is reversible but the chronic amnestic phase (Korsakoff syndrome) is often permanent"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This patient has **Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome**, caused by **thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency** — most commonly due to chronic alcohol use with malnutrition, but also seen in elderly patients with malabsorption, prolonged vomiting, or anorexia. **Wernicke encephalopathy** (acute phase) presents with the classic triad: (1) **Encephalopathy** (confusion, altered mental status); (2) **Oculomotor dysfunction** (nystagmus, lateral rectus palsy/CN VI, conjugate gaze palsy); (3) **Cerebellar ataxia** (wide-based, unsteady gait). Only ~16% present with the complete triad. **Korsakoff syndrome** (chronic phase) develops if Wernicke''''s is untreated or inadequately treated: **anterograde amnesia** (cannot form new memories), **retrograde amnesia**, and **confabulation** (filling memory gaps with fabricated stories without awareness). CRITICAL: **IV thiamine MUST be given BEFORE glucose**. Glucose metabolism requires thiamine as a cofactor; administering glucose to a thiamine-depleted patient diverts the remaining thiamine stores, potentially precipitating or worsening Wernicke encephalopathy. Wernicke''''s is **reversible** with prompt IV thiamine; Korsakoff''''s is often **irreversible** (~20% recovery). (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
