    (v_topic_id, 'A 69-year-old male with a history of chronic alcohol use disorder and poor nutritional status presents with acute confusion, horizontal nystagmus, bilateral lateral rectus palsy (CN VI), and unsteady gait. After treatment of the acute phase, the patient develops severe anterograde amnesia (unable to form new memories) and begins **confabulating** (fabricating elaborate stories to fill memory gaps without awareness of doing so). Which of the following is the most important initial treatment, and what complication must be avoided?', '["IV thiamine (vitamin B1) must be administered BEFORE any glucose-containing fluids \u2014 glucose metabolism consumes thiamine, and giving glucose first can precipitate or worsen Wernicke encephalopathy; the acute phase (Wernicke) is reversible but the chronic amnestic phase (Korsakoff syndrome) is often permanent", "IV dextrose 50% (D50) should be given first to treat presumed hypoglycemia, followed by oral thiamine supplementation at discharge", "Oral folate supplementation alone is sufficient to reverse both the acute encephalopathy and the chronic amnesia", "High-dose IV corticosteroids to reduce cerebral edema from alcohol-related neuroinflammation", "Emergent MRI-guided brain biopsy of the mammillary bodies to confirm the diagnosis before initiating any treatment"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This patient has **Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome**, caused by **thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency** — most commonly due to chronic alcohol use with malnutrition, but also seen in elderly patients with malabsorption, prolonged vomiting, or anorexia. **Wernicke encephalopathy** (acute phase) presents with the classic triad: (1) **Encephalopathy** (confusion, altered mental status); (2) **Oculomotor dysfunction** (nystagmus, lateral rectus palsy/CN VI, conjugate gaze palsy); (3) **Cerebellar ataxia** (wide-based, unsteady gait). Only ~16% present with the complete triad. **Korsakoff syndrome** (chronic phase) develops if Wernicke''''s is untreated or inadequately treated: **anterograde amnesia** (cannot form new memories), **retrograde amnesia**, and **confabulation** (filling memory gaps with fabricated stories without awareness). CRITICAL: **IV thiamine MUST be given BEFORE glucose**. Glucose metabolism requires thiamine as a cofactor; administering glucose to a thiamine-depleted patient diverts the remaining thiamine stores, potentially precipitating or worsening Wernicke encephalopathy. Wernicke''''s is **reversible** with prompt IV thiamine; Korsakoff''''s is often **irreversible** (~20% recovery). (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 77-year-old female with mild Alzheimer''''s disease is started on **Rivastigmine 6 mg** daily. Three weeks later, the patient presents with nausea, diarrhea, abdominal cramping, and a heart rate of **53 bpm**. Which of the following best explains the mechanism of action of this drug class and its adverse effect profile?', '["These drugs are NMDA receptor antagonists that cause glutamate-mediated excitotoxicity leading to GI disturbance and cardiac arrhythmias", "These drugs are selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs) and the GI symptoms represent serotonin syndrome", "Cholinesterase inhibitors (donepezil, galantamine, rivastigmine) inhibit acetylcholinesterase, increasing acetylcholine at synapses; peripheral cholinergic excess causes GI symptoms (nausea, vomiting, diarrhea) and vagotonic bradycardia; the Beers Criteria cautions use in patients with bradycardia, sick sinus syndrome, or syncope history", "These drugs block muscarinic receptors, causing anticholinergic toxicity (tachycardia, urinary retention, dry mouth, constipation)", "These drugs are dopamine agonists that cause peripheral vasodilation and reflex bradycardia"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Cholinesterase inhibitors (ChEIs)** — donepezil, galantamine, and rivastigmine — are first-line pharmacotherapy for **mild-to-moderate Alzheimer''''s disease**. **Mechanism**: Inhibit acetylcholinesterase (AChE), preventing breakdown of acetylcholine (ACh) in the synaptic cleft, thereby increasing cholinergic neurotransmission. In AD, there is a deficit of ACh due to loss of cholinergic neurons in the nucleus basalis of Meynert. **Adverse effects** are due to peripheral cholinergic (parasympathetic) excess: (1) **GI**: nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, anorexia, abdominal cramps (most common, ~20%); (2) **Cardiovascular**: **bradycardia**, AV block, syncope (vagotonic effect); (3) **Other**: vivid dreams, insomnia (donepezil), muscle cramps. The **Beers Criteria** warns: use ChEIs with caution in patients with **bradycardia, sick sinus syndrome, cardiac conduction abnormalities, or history of syncope/falls**. Rivastigmine patch formulation reduces GI side effects compared to oral forms. All ChEIs provide modest symptomatic benefit (~1-2 point improvement on MMSE) but do NOT alter disease progression. (Ref: AGS Beers Criteria 2023; Hazzard''''s Ch. 72)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old female with mild Alzheimer''''s disease is started on **Donepezil 5 mg** daily. Three weeks later, the patient presents with nausea, diarrhea, abdominal cramping, and a heart rate of **54 bpm**. Which of the following best explains the mechanism of action of this drug class and its adverse effect profile?', '["These drugs block muscarinic receptors, causing anticholinergic toxicity (tachycardia, urinary retention, dry mouth, constipation)", "These drugs are NMDA receptor antagonists that cause glutamate-mediated excitotoxicity leading to GI disturbance and cardiac arrhythmias", "These drugs are selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs) and the GI symptoms represent serotonin syndrome", "These drugs are dopamine agonists that cause peripheral vasodilation and reflex bradycardia", "Cholinesterase inhibitors (donepezil, galantamine, rivastigmine) inhibit acetylcholinesterase, increasing acetylcholine at synapses; peripheral cholinergic excess causes GI symptoms (nausea, vomiting, diarrhea) and vagotonic bradycardia; the Beers Criteria cautions use in patients with bradycardia, sick sinus syndrome, or syncope history"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Cholinesterase inhibitors (ChEIs)** — donepezil, galantamine, and rivastigmine — are first-line pharmacotherapy for **mild-to-moderate Alzheimer''''s disease**. **Mechanism**: Inhibit acetylcholinesterase (AChE), preventing breakdown of acetylcholine (ACh) in the synaptic cleft, thereby increasing cholinergic neurotransmission. In AD, there is a deficit of ACh due to loss of cholinergic neurons in the nucleus basalis of Meynert. **Adverse effects** are due to peripheral cholinergic (parasympathetic) excess: (1) **GI**: nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, anorexia, abdominal cramps (most common, ~20%); (2) **Cardiovascular**: **bradycardia**, AV block, syncope (vagotonic effect); (3) **Other**: vivid dreams, insomnia (donepezil), muscle cramps. The **Beers Criteria** warns: use ChEIs with caution in patients with **bradycardia, sick sinus syndrome, cardiac conduction abnormalities, or history of syncope/falls**. Rivastigmine patch formulation reduces GI side effects compared to oral forms. All ChEIs provide modest symptomatic benefit (~1-2 point improvement on MMSE) but do NOT alter disease progression. (Ref: AGS Beers Criteria 2023; Hazzard''''s Ch. 72)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 86-year-old female with mild Alzheimer''''s disease is started on **Galantamine 16 mg** daily. Three weeks later, the patient presents with nausea, diarrhea, abdominal cramping, and a heart rate of **46 bpm**. Which of the following best explains the mechanism of action of this drug class and its adverse effect profile?', '["These drugs block muscarinic receptors, causing anticholinergic toxicity (tachycardia, urinary retention, dry mouth, constipation)", "Cholinesterase inhibitors (donepezil, galantamine, rivastigmine) inhibit acetylcholinesterase, increasing acetylcholine at synapses; peripheral cholinergic excess causes GI symptoms (nausea, vomiting, diarrhea) and vagotonic bradycardia; the Beers Criteria cautions use in patients with bradycardia, sick sinus syndrome, or syncope history", "These drugs are selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs) and the GI symptoms represent serotonin syndrome", "These drugs are NMDA receptor antagonists that cause glutamate-mediated excitotoxicity leading to GI disturbance and cardiac arrhythmias", "These drugs are dopamine agonists that cause peripheral vasodilation and reflex bradycardia"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Cholinesterase inhibitors (ChEIs)** — donepezil, galantamine, and rivastigmine — are first-line pharmacotherapy for **mild-to-moderate Alzheimer''''s disease**. **Mechanism**: Inhibit acetylcholinesterase (AChE), preventing breakdown of acetylcholine (ACh) in the synaptic cleft, thereby increasing cholinergic neurotransmission. In AD, there is a deficit of ACh due to loss of cholinergic neurons in the nucleus basalis of Meynert. **Adverse effects** are due to peripheral cholinergic (parasympathetic) excess: (1) **GI**: nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, anorexia, abdominal cramps (most common, ~20%); (2) **Cardiovascular**: **bradycardia**, AV block, syncope (vagotonic effect); (3) **Other**: vivid dreams, insomnia (donepezil), muscle cramps. The **Beers Criteria** warns: use ChEIs with caution in patients with **bradycardia, sick sinus syndrome, cardiac conduction abnormalities, or history of syncope/falls**. Rivastigmine patch formulation reduces GI side effects compared to oral forms. All ChEIs provide modest symptomatic benefit (~1-2 point improvement on MMSE) but do NOT alter disease progression. (Ref: AGS Beers Criteria 2023; Hazzard''''s Ch. 72)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 88-year-old female with mild Alzheimer''''s disease is started on **Rivastigmine 9.5 mg** daily. Three weeks later, the patient presents with nausea, diarrhea, abdominal cramping, and a heart rate of **54 bpm**. Which of the following best explains the mechanism of action of this drug class and its adverse effect profile?', '["Cholinesterase inhibitors (donepezil, galantamine, rivastigmine) inhibit acetylcholinesterase, increasing acetylcholine at synapses; peripheral cholinergic excess causes GI symptoms (nausea, vomiting, diarrhea) and vagotonic bradycardia; the Beers Criteria cautions use in patients with bradycardia, sick sinus syndrome, or syncope history", "These drugs are NMDA receptor antagonists that cause glutamate-mediated excitotoxicity leading to GI disturbance and cardiac arrhythmias", "These drugs are dopamine agonists that cause peripheral vasodilation and reflex bradycardia", "These drugs block muscarinic receptors, causing anticholinergic toxicity (tachycardia, urinary retention, dry mouth, constipation)", "These drugs are selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs) and the GI symptoms represent serotonin syndrome"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Cholinesterase inhibitors (ChEIs)** — donepezil, galantamine, and rivastigmine — are first-line pharmacotherapy for **mild-to-moderate Alzheimer''''s disease**. **Mechanism**: Inhibit acetylcholinesterase (AChE), preventing breakdown of acetylcholine (ACh) in the synaptic cleft, thereby increasing cholinergic neurotransmission. In AD, there is a deficit of ACh due to loss of cholinergic neurons in the nucleus basalis of Meynert. **Adverse effects** are due to peripheral cholinergic (parasympathetic) excess: (1) **GI**: nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, anorexia, abdominal cramps (most common, ~20%); (2) **Cardiovascular**: **bradycardia**, AV block, syncope (vagotonic effect); (3) **Other**: vivid dreams, insomnia (donepezil), muscle cramps. The **Beers Criteria** warns: use ChEIs with caution in patient