h **bradycardia, sick sinus syndrome, cardiac conduction abnormalities, or history of syncope/falls**. Rivastigmine patch formulation reduces GI side effects compared to oral forms. All ChEIs provide modest symptomatic benefit (~1-2 point improvement on MMSE) but do NOT alter disease progression. (Ref: AGS Beers Criteria 2023; Hazzard''''s Ch. 72)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 71-year-old woman with mild Alzheimer''''s disease is started on **Rivastigmine 6 mg** daily. Three weeks later, the patient presents with nausea, diarrhea, abdominal cramping, and a heart rate of **47 bpm**. Which of the following best explains the mechanism of action of this drug class and its adverse effect profile?', '["These drugs are NMDA receptor antagonists that cause glutamate-mediated excitotoxicity leading to GI disturbance and cardiac arrhythmias", "These drugs block muscarinic receptors, causing anticholinergic toxicity (tachycardia, urinary retention, dry mouth, constipation)", "These drugs are dopamine agonists that cause peripheral vasodilation and reflex bradycardia", "These drugs are selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs) and the GI symptoms represent serotonin syndrome", "Cholinesterase inhibitors (donepezil, galantamine, rivastigmine) inhibit acetylcholinesterase, increasing acetylcholine at synapses; peripheral cholinergic excess causes GI symptoms (nausea, vomiting, diarrhea) and vagotonic bradycardia; the Beers Criteria cautions use in patients with bradycardia, sick sinus syndrome, or syncope history"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Cholinesterase inhibitors (ChEIs)** — donepezil, galantamine, and rivastigmine — are first-line pharmacotherapy for **mild-to-moderate Alzheimer''''s disease**. **Mechanism**: Inhibit acetylcholinesterase (AChE), preventing breakdown of acetylcholine (ACh) in the synaptic cleft, thereby increasing cholinergic neurotransmission. In AD, there is a deficit of ACh due to loss of cholinergic neurons in the nucleus basalis of Meynert. **Adverse effects** are due to peripheral cholinergic (parasympathetic) excess: (1) **GI**: nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, anorexia, abdominal cramps (most common, ~20%); (2) **Cardiovascular**: **bradycardia**, AV block, syncope (vagotonic effect); (3) **Other**: vivid dreams, insomnia (donepezil), muscle cramps. The **Beers Criteria** warns: use ChEIs with caution in patients with **bradycardia, sick sinus syndrome, cardiac conduction abnormalities, or history of syncope/falls**. Rivastigmine patch formulation reduces GI side effects compared to oral forms. All ChEIs provide modest symptomatic benefit (~1-2 point improvement on MMSE) but do NOT alter disease progression. (Ref: AGS Beers Criteria 2023; Hazzard''''s Ch. 72)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 86-year-old man with moderate-to-severe Alzheimer''''s disease is currently on donepezil. The family asks about adding memantine. Which of the following CORRECTLY describes memantine''''s mechanism of action, indication, and evidence for combination therapy?', '["Memantine is a GABA-B receptor agonist that reduces neuronal excitability; it is indicated for all forms of dementia including vascular, Lewy body, and frontotemporal", "Memantine is an amyloid-beta monoclonal antibody that clears senile plaques; it is indicated only for early-stage AD with confirmed amyloid pathology on PET", "Memantine is a cholinesterase inhibitor that works synergistically with donepezil by inhibiting butyrylcholinesterase; it is indicated as first-line monotherapy for mild AD", "Memantine is a low-affinity, uncompetitive NMDA receptor antagonist that blocks pathological tonic glutamate signaling (excitotoxicity) while preserving physiological synaptic transmission; it is indicated ONLY for moderate-to-severe AD (not mild AD or MCI); combination with a cholinesterase inhibitor provides modest additional benefit over monotherapy", "Memantine is a high-affinity competitive NMDA agonist that enhances glutamate signaling to improve synaptic plasticity; it is indicated for all stages of AD including MCI"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Memantine** is a **low-affinity, voltage-dependent, uncompetitive NMDA receptor antagonist**. In AD, chronic **glutamate excitotoxicity** occurs: excessive tonic activation of NMDA receptors leads to sustained calcium influx, causing synaptic dysfunction and neuronal death. Memantine''''s unique mechanism: it preferentially blocks these **pathological tonic glutamate signals** (low-level, sustained) while **preserving normal physiological synaptic transmission** (brief, high-level glutamate bursts needed for learning and memory). This is possible because of its **low affinity and rapid off-rate** — it is displaced from the channel by the larger, faster glutamate signals of normal neurotransmission. **Clinical indication**: **Moderate-to-severe AD only** (MMSE ≤20 or CDR ≥2). It is NOT indicated for mild AD or MCI — clinical trials showed no significant benefit in these populations. **Combination therapy**: Memantine + cholinesterase inhibitor (e.g., donepezil) provides modest additional benefits in cognition, ADLs, and global function compared to ChEI alone in moderate-to-severe AD. Common side effects: dizziness, headache, constipation, confusion (generally well-tolerated). (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; Master the Boards Step 2 CK; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 79-year-old woman with moderate-to-severe Alzheimer''''s disease is currently on donepezil. The family asks about adding memantine. Which of the following CORRECTLY describes memantine''''s mechanism of action, indication, and evidence for combination therapy?', '["Memantine is a high-affinity competitive NMDA agonist that enhances glutamate signaling to improve synaptic plasticity; it is indicated for all stages of AD including MCI", "Memantine is a GABA-B receptor agonist that reduces neuronal excitability; it is indicated for all forms of dementia including vascular, Lewy body, and frontotemporal", "Memantine is an amyloid-beta monoclonal antibody that clears senile plaques; it is indicated only for early-stage AD with confirmed amyloid pathology on PET", "Memantine is a cholinesterase inhibitor that works synergistically with donepezil by inhibiting butyrylcholinesterase; it is indicated as first-line monotherapy for mild AD", "Memantine is a low-affinity, uncompetitive NMDA receptor antagonist that blocks pathological tonic glutamate signaling (excitotoxicity) while preserving physiological synaptic transmission; it is indicated ONLY for moderate-to-severe AD (not mild AD or MCI); combination with a cholinesterase inhibitor provides modest additional benefit over monotherapy"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Memantine** is a **low-affinity, voltage-dependent, uncompetitive NMDA receptor antagonist**. In AD, chronic **glutamate excitotoxicity** occurs: excessive tonic activation of NMDA receptors leads to sustained calcium influx, causing synaptic dysfunction and neuronal death. Memantine''''s unique mechanism: it preferentially blocks these **pathological tonic glutamate signals** (low-level, sustained) while **preserving normal physiological synaptic transmission** (brief, high-level glutamate bursts needed for learning and memory). This is possible because of its **low affinity and rapid off-rate** — it is displaced from the channel by the larger, faster glutamate signals of normal neurotransmission. **Clinical indication**: **Moderate-to-severe AD only** (MMSE ≤20 or CDR ≥2). It is NOT indicated for mild AD or MCI — clinical trials showed no significant benefit in these populations. **Combination therapy**: Memantine + cholinesterase inhibitor (e.g., donepezil) provides modest additional benefits in cognition, ADLs, and global function compared to ChEI alone in moderate-to-severe AD. Common side effects: dizziness, headache, constipation, confusion (generally well-tolerated). (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; Master the Boards Step 2 CK; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
