    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-year-old woman with moderate-to-severe Alzheimer''''s disease is currently on donepezil. The family asks about adding memantine. Which of the following CORRECTLY describes memantine''''s mechanism of action, indication, and evidence for combination therapy?', '["Memantine is a GABA-B receptor agonist that reduces neuronal excitability; it is indicated for all forms of dementia including vascular, Lewy body, and frontotemporal", "Memantine is a cholinesterase inhibitor that works synergistically with donepezil by inhibiting butyrylcholinesterase; it is indicated as first-line monotherapy for mild AD", "Memantine is an amyloid-beta monoclonal antibody that clears senile plaques; it is indicated only for early-stage AD with confirmed amyloid pathology on PET", "Memantine is a low-affinity, uncompetitive NMDA receptor antagonist that blocks pathological tonic glutamate signaling (excitotoxicity) while preserving physiological synaptic transmission; it is indicated ONLY for moderate-to-severe AD (not mild AD or MCI); combination with a cholinesterase inhibitor provides modest additional benefit over monotherapy", "Memantine is a high-affinity competitive NMDA agonist that enhances glutamate signaling to improve synaptic plasticity; it is indicated for all stages of AD including MCI"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Memantine** is a **low-affinity, voltage-dependent, uncompetitive NMDA receptor antagonist**. In AD, chronic **glutamate excitotoxicity** occurs: excessive tonic activation of NMDA receptors leads to sustained calcium influx, causing synaptic dysfunction and neuronal death. Memantine''''s unique mechanism: it preferentially blocks these **pathological tonic glutamate signals** (low-level, sustained) while **preserving normal physiological synaptic transmission** (brief, high-level glutamate bursts needed for learning and memory). This is possible because of its **low affinity and rapid off-rate** — it is displaced from the channel by the larger, faster g