    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old woman with early-stage Alzheimer''''s disease confirmed by positive amyloid PET scan asks about newer anti-amyloid immunotherapy. The physician discusses lecanemab. Which of the following CORRECTLY describes this medication, its mechanism, and the most important adverse effect to monitor?', '["Lecanemab is a humanized monoclonal antibody that targets soluble amyloid-beta protofibrils, reducing amyloid plaque burden; it modestly slows cognitive decline in early AD; the most important adverse effect is ARIA (Amyloid-Related Imaging Abnormalities) \u2014 cerebral edema (ARIA-E) and microhemorrhages (ARIA-H) \u2014 requiring regular MRI monitoring; APOE \u03b54 homozygotes have the highest ARIA risk", "Lecanemab is a small-molecule beta-secretase (BACE) inhibitor taken orally that completely halts amyloid production and reverses cognitive decline in all stages of AD", "Lecanemab is a tau protein vaccine that prevents neurofibrillary tangle formation; it is indicated for severe AD and has no significant side effects", "Lecanemab is an NMDA receptor antagonist antibody that blocks glutamate excitotoxicity; it requires no imaging monitoring and is safe in all APOE genotypes", "Lecanemab is a cholinesterase inhibitor delivered via monthly intrathecal injection that provides superior efficacy to oral donepezil"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Lecanemab (Leqembi)** is a humanized IgG1 monoclonal antibody that targets **soluble amyloid-beta (Aβ) protofibrils** — the most toxic form of aggregated amyloid. It received **full FDA approval** in 2023 based on the CLARITY-AD trial, which showed it reduced amyloid plaque burden and **slowed cognitive decline by ~27%** over 18 months in early symptomatic AD (MCI due to AD or mild AD dementia). Key points: (1) **Indication**: Early-stage AD with **confirmed amyloid pathology** (positive amyloid PET or CSF biomarkers); NOT indicated for moderate-severe AD; (2) **Administration**: IV infusion every 2 weeks; (3) **ARIA (Amyloid-Related Imaging Abnormalities)**: The most important adverse effect — **ARIA-E** (vasogenic edema, ~13%) and **ARIA-H** (microhemorrhages/superficial siderosis, ~17%). Most ARIA is asymptomatic but can cause headache, confusion, or seizures. Requires **regular MRI monitoring** (baseline, then periodically); (4) **APOE ε4 homozygotes** have the highest ARIA risk (~35%); APOE genotyping is recommended before treatment; (5) Anticoagulant use increases bleeding risk with ARIA-H. (Ref: CLARITY-AD Trial; FDA Prescribing Information; Hazzard''''s Ch. 72)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 74-year-old man with early-stage Alzheimer''''s disease confirmed by positive amyloid PET scan asks about newer anti-amyloid immunotherapy. The physician discusses lecanemab. Which of the following CORRECTLY describes this medication, its mechanism, and the most important adverse effect to monitor?', '["Lecanemab is a cholinesterase inhibitor delivered via monthly intrathecal injection that provides superior efficacy to oral donepezil", "Lecanemab is a tau protein vaccine that prevents neurofibrillary tangle formation; it is indicated for severe AD and has no significant side effects", "Lecanemab is an NMDA receptor antagonist antibody that blocks glutamate excitotoxicity; it requires no imaging monitoring and is safe in all APOE genotypes", "Lecanemab is a humanized monoclonal antibody that targets soluble amyloid-beta protofibrils, reducing amyloid plaque burden; it modestly slows cognitive decline in early AD; the most important adverse effect is ARIA (Amyloid-Related Imaging Abnormalities) \u2014 cerebral edema (ARIA-E) and microhemorrhages (ARIA-H) \u2014 requiring regular MRI monitoring; APOE \u03b54 homozygotes have the highest ARIA risk", "Lecanemab is a small-molecule beta-secretase (BACE) inhibitor taken orally that completely halts amyloid production and reverses cognitive decline in all stages of AD"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Lecanemab (Leqembi)** is a humanized IgG1 monoclonal antibody that targets **soluble amyloid-beta (Aβ) protofibrils** — the most toxic form of aggregated amyloid. It received **full FDA approval** in 2023 based on the CLARITY-AD trial, which showed it reduced amyloid plaque burden and **slowed cognitive decline by ~27%** over 18 months in early symptomatic AD (MCI due to AD or mild AD dementia). Key points: (1) **Indication**: Early-stage AD with **confirmed amyloid pathology** (positive amyloid PET or CSF biomarkers); NOT indicated for moderate-severe AD; (2) **Administration**: IV infusion every 2 weeks; (3) **ARIA (Amyl